/// Render-distance and fog configuration.
///
/// RENDER_DIST is the one render-range knob, in world `u`: how far ground
/// geometry exists, and where fog finishes hiding its edge. Everything else
/// about range is derived from it — the chunks that mount, the pages fetched,
/// the pages evicted (terrain/config.json).
///
/// It replaces three independently chosen numbers: a fog distance in world
/// units and two page radii in whole pages. Because both page radii keyed on
/// the page the view centre stood in, the delivered range was not the stated
/// one — geometry ended anywhere between one and two pages out depending on
/// where in its page the camera stood, and crossing a boundary moved the
/// boundary by a whole page. See issue/20260912-what-to-render-when.md.
///
/// Ranges are measured from the view centre, not from the camera, because
/// residency has to be invariant under rotation: at a wide zoom the camera
/// sits up to ~1023u from that centre, and following it would sweep pages in
/// and out of the cache on a spin. Which tier a chunk shows and which chunk is
/// built first are camera-relative and always were — both measure the chunk's
/// own 3D bounds centre against the camera, so content standing 100u above the
/// ground is ranked where it actually is.
///
/// Anchoring the range at the centre is only sufficient because the camera
/// always looks at it, which keeps the visible wedge nearby. Measured over
/// every zoom, tilt, aspect ratio and pivot height the camera allows, visible
/// ground reaches at most ~1532u from the centre — the worst case being a
/// close zoom onto something standing above the terrain, where the camera
/// rises with it and sees almost a full fog radius in every direction.
/// CHUNK_RENDER_DIST is 1664u, two chunks past the range rather than one, so
/// the margin (132u) also covers the anchor's own chunk quantization (up to
/// ~90u). Widening the field of view, or letting the camera look away from
/// its pivot, is what would make the margin too small.
///
/// 1536u is chosen against what it costs to hold. Chunks mount two chunks past
/// it (CHUNK_RENDER_DIST, 1664u), which touches 21 pages at worst; the pages
/// actually held are the nearest 32 (~129MB decoded), against the 49 pages
/// (~197MB) that discarded the tab on an iPhone 13 mini. It is wider than the
/// 1024u that was really guaranteed before, and it is the same everywhere
/// rather than only at the middle of a page.
pub mod render_cfg {
    /// VIEW_DIST * 1.5. VIEW_DIST (camera/config.json) is sized past
    /// CHUNK_RENDER_DIST below, the real edge of mounted terrain, so FAR_CLIP
    /// keeps margin over where geometry stops existing rather than being an
    /// arbitrary multiple. The fog gradient is FOG_NEAR/FOG_FAR (linear, in
    /// shade.wgsl, from the theme through environment.rs), and it ends exactly
    /// where geometry does, so ground always fades into the fog colour instead
    /// of being cut off while still visible. The sky is drawn unfogged
    /// (sky.wgsl), so the horizon still reads as a full vista — only ground
    /// geometry fades.
    pub const FAR_CLIP: f64 = 6144.0;
    /// RENDER_DIST * 0.42. Haze starts here and is total at RENDER_DIST, where
    /// geometry stops. The fraction is the one the previous pair had, so the
    /// near edge sits where it did; what changes is that the far edge is now a
    /// distance geometry actually reaches, so the fade completes instead of
    /// being cut.
    pub const FOG_NEAR: f64 = 645.12;
    /// RENDER_DIST.
    pub const FOG_FAR: f64 = 1536.0;
    pub const RENDER_DIST: f64 = 1536.0;
    /// RENDER_DIST + 2 * CHUNK_CELL_SPAN * U_PER_CELL. How far out a chunk is
    /// actually mounted. Which pages exist to mount from is a count rather than
    /// a distance (terrain/config.json's RESIDENT_PAGES). Two chunks past the
    /// range: one so a chunk's far edge does not visibly clip mid-fade at the
    /// cutoff, and one for the margin the invariant above needs. 21 pages at
    /// worst (~85MB decoded).
    pub const CHUNK_RENDER_DIST: f64 = 1664.0;
    pub const KEYS: &[&str] = &["FAR_CLIP", "FOG_NEAR", "FOG_FAR", "RENDER_DIST", "CHUNK_RENDER_DIST"];
}

/// Minimum-brightness floor shared by every lit surface — terrain and
/// placed cells, decor, actors — through the one shading source
/// (crates/pxl/core/src/render/shader/shade.wgsl, `frame.floor`, carried
/// in the theme through environment.rs). One shared constant, so terrain
/// and objects cannot dim apart. Tune this one number to retune every
/// surface's night floor at once.
pub mod light_cfg {
    /// Judged on a real device, not a desktop monitor — a darker floor that read
    /// as convincing night on a monitor was simply too dark to see the world on
    /// a phone screen.
    ///
    /// Raising this alone flattens the scene, since it lifts every face by the
    /// same amount and erases the shading that carries depth. It moves together
    /// with the moon's directional light, which adds
    /// brightness that still differs face to face.
    pub const FLOOR: f64 = 0.6;
    pub const KEYS: &[&str] = &["FLOOR"];
}

/// Fresnel/rim highlight, applied uniformly to everything lit — terrain,
/// placed objects, decor and actors alike, since one shading source lights
/// them (crates/pxl/core/src/render/shader/shade.wgsl) — a thin bright edge on
/// faces that graze the view direction, the classic "toy block catching
/// light at its silhouette" cue. Originally object-only (meant to help
/// placed objects read as distinct from terrain cells sharing the same
/// block palette), but a shared render pipeline shouldn't treat one
/// geometry role differently from another for the same shader term, so it
/// applies everywhere show_rim_light is on. The falloff's exponent is the
/// shader's; this is the linear scale on it, the one that controls peak
/// brightness, written into the frame uniform.
pub mod rim_cfg {
    pub const INTENSITY: f64 = 0.1;
    pub const KEYS: &[&str] = &["INTENSITY"];
}

